msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Coverage means bulletins the product ingests or links to, not map coverage, "
"which is usually wider. Prices are annual unless stated."
msgstr ""

msgid "Feature matrix"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Thirteen things people actually choose on. Read the dots with the caveat in "
"the legend: a product marked <em>not established</em> may well do the thing "
"— it means our sources do not say, usually because that product was checked "
"for coverage and price rather than interrogated feature by feature. Reading "
"those as a “no” would invent an advantage out of a gap in our own notes."
msgstr ""

msgid "Feature matrix: which of thirteen features each avalanche app offers."
msgstr ""

msgid "Feature"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Competitor rows come from the sources listed under “how this was checked”. "
"The Snowdesk column is checked against our own source code, which is the "
"only column we can check that way."
msgstr ""

msgid "What a feature table cannot show you"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A table like the one above counts what a product has, so it rewards the app "
"with the most in it. That is worth saying out loud on a page that leads with "
"one, because it misses the thing you notice first when you actually open "
"these apps: how much of each one is not the forecast."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Most of them are platforms. There are modules and tabs, a learning section, "
"a shop, a route library, a community feed, a prompt to upgrade, and in one "
"case adverts. All of that is doing a job for the people who built it, and "
"some of it is genuinely good — WhiteRisk's teaching material is the best in "
"the category by a wide margin. But it sits between you and the number you "
"opened the app to read, on a phone, outside, in a lift queue, wearing gloves."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Snowdesk does one thing: a map, and the forecast for wherever you touch it. "
"There is no learning module, no shop, no upgrade prompt, no advertising, and "
"nothing to buy. You do not need an account to read a bulletin, and there is "
"no app to install before you can see anything."
msgstr ""

msgid "The honest half of that"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Restraint is a trade, not a free win. The things we leave out are things "
"other people want. If you are learning to read a bulletin, WhiteRisk will "
"teach you properly and we will not. If you want a library of routes to pick "
"from, Whympr has a hundred thousand and we have none — we hold the one you "
"bring us. If you want the day's outings from people who were just out there, "
"that is Whympr too."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"And the same scepticism this page applies elsewhere applies here: an app "
"with nothing to sell you also has no business model, which is a fair thing "
"to hold against it. We note above that AvalancheClarity has no visible way "
"of paying for itself and that this is worth knowing before you depend on it. "
"That is exactly as true of us."
msgstr ""

msgid "Planning together, and your own content"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Reading the forecast is one job. Deciding a route, agreeing a meeting point "
"and getting four people to the same trailhead at the same time is another, "
"and it is the dimension these products differ on most while advertising it "
"least. Two are worth separating: whether you can bring your <em>own</em> "
"route into the app, and whether anyone else can see the plan."
msgstr ""

msgid "Bringing your own route"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"<strong class=\"text-text-1\">Whympr</strong> and <strong class=\"text-"
"text-1\">Snowdesk</strong> both take a route you supply. <strong "
"class=\"text-text-1\">WhiteRisk</strong> lets you draw one and keeps a "
"personal tour library that syncs between the web and the app, which is the "
"same idea reached a different way. <strong class=\"text-"
"text-1\">Skitourenguru</strong> and <strong class=\"text-text-1\">Yéti</"
"strong> work the opposite way round: their value is a curated database of "
"about 13,000 tours that they score for you, so the route is theirs, not "
"yours. <strong class=\"text-text-1\">SnowSafe</strong> states plainly that "
"it is not a planner."
msgstr ""

msgid "Sharing the plan with other people"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This is where the list thins out. <strong class=\"text-text-1\">Whympr</"
"strong> has by far the richest community layer — trip reports carrying "
"current conditions, an activity wall of the last ten days of outings, GPS "
"tracking and a logbook — but it is a feed of what people <em>did</em>, "
"published after the fact, rather than a plan for a day that has not happened "
"yet."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"<strong class=\"text-text-1\">Snowdesk</strong> is the only product here "
"where a trip is a shared object: a route on a named day, at a stated time "
"and meeting point, behind one link anybody can open and read before deciding "
"anything. Saving it to your own trips needs an account, and everyone who "
"saves it reads the same trip rather than a copy, so when the organiser moves "
"the time or the meeting point, nobody is left on a stale plan. No equivalent "
"was found in any other product checked — though see the note on what that "
"does and does not prove, below."
msgstr ""

msgid "Reporting what you saw"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"<strong class=\"text-text-1\">WhiteRisk</strong> added a crowd-sourced form "
"for marking an avalanche's starting point and logging its size and type. "
"<strong class=\"text-text-1\">Whympr</strong> takes field reports without a "
"login at all, and expires them after 90 days unless another user "
"corroborates them, which is a neat answer to stale conditions reports. "
"<strong class=\"text-text-1\">Snowdesk</strong> takes field observations and "
"shows them on the map. The other products in this list are read-only."
msgstr ""

msgid "The products in detail"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Grouped by what each product is fundamentally for. A bulletin app and a trip "
"planner that happens to show a bulletin are different purchases, and "
"comparing them feature-for-feature flatters the wrong one."
msgstr ""

msgid "The official one"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"WSL Institute for Snow and Avalanche Research SLF · web and mobile, four "
"languages · checked 30 Aug 2026"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Not a third party interpreting an official product — it <em>is</em> the "
"official product, from the institute that writes the Swiss bulletin. Four "
"modules: an avalanche knowledge base, structured e-learning with per-lesson "
"quizzes, a tour planner, and professional tooling for instructors. The "
"planner is the part that matters most: draw a route and WhiteRisk marks the "
"critical passages against terrain and avalanche information, with an "
"elevation profile and estimated duration for alternatives."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The 2025/26 redesign added the French bulletin outright, a crowd-sourced "
"form for logging an avalanche seen from the field, and a clearer display of "
"critical passages. Maps cover Switzerland, France and Austria with slope-"
"angle and avalanche-terrain layers; the free tier drops to OpenStreetMap. "
"Readings from the automated measuring-station network are free."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"<strong class=\"text-text-1\">Strongest at</strong> authority, terrain-aware "
"route analysis, station data, and the deepest educational material in the "
"category by a distance. <strong class=\"text-text-1\">Weakest at</strong> "
"reach beyond Switzerland — Austria and Italy get map coverage but not a "
"first-class bulletin — and it is a platform with modules, licences and an "
"account, which is more than some people want when they are checking one "
"number in a lift queue."
msgstr ""

msgid "Bulletin-first apps"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"First Line Solutions GmbH, Austria · iOS and Android, no web surface · "
"checked 30 Aug 2026"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Explicitly not a planner. The design principle its makers state — as simple "
"and reliable to operate as a transceiver — shows in the product. Coverage is "
"the Austrian avalanche warning services plus Bavaria, Alto Adige/Südtirol, "
"Veneto, Trentino, Slovenia, Slovakia, Czechia, Poland, Iceland and Norway. "
"No Switzerland and no France."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Its most recent release added push notifications for danger levels and a "
"weather-station map layer with interactive charts. Alongside those sit a "
"clinometer for measuring a slope, an aspect compass pairing slope aspect "
"with the day's danger patterns, mountain weather forecasts, and offline "
"reading of bulletins synced while you had signal."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"<strong class=\"text-text-1\">Strongest at</strong> alerting on rising "
"danger and on a mid-day re-issue, station data, and field instruments in the "
"same app as the forecast. <strong class=\"text-text-1\">Weakest at</strong> "
"everything around the bulletin: no map-first entry point, no history or "
"season view, no route handling, and nothing on the web. The bulletin itself "
"is never paywalled, which is the right call."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Simon Perry, a Tignes-based guidebook author · app plus an embeddable widget "
"· checked 30 Aug 2026, secondary sources only"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The broadest cross-border coverage of anything in this list, built by one "
"person rather than an institution. It translates the official bulletin, "
"explains it in plain language, and pushes a notification the moment a new "
"bulletin or a revision publishes for a region you follow — the only product "
"here that alerts on a re-issue rather than only on a danger level."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Translation depth is uneven, and this is the thing to check before relying "
"on it. Switzerland gets all 134 micro-regions in eight languages and France "
"gets all 36 massifs, but Austria, Italy, Germany, Norway, Sweden, Spain, "
"Andorra, Slovenia, Slovakia, Czechia, Finland and Scotland get a map link to "
"the official bulletin rather than a translated one."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"<strong class=\"text-text-1\">Strongest at</strong> reading a foreign-"
"language bulletin in Switzerland or France, and knowing the instant one "
"changes. <strong class=\"text-text-1\">Weakest at</strong> everywhere else, "
"where it is a link rather than a translation. No terrain or route tooling, "
"and no stated business model — worth knowing for something you would come to "
"depend on."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The publisher of this page — read the disclosure at the foot · web, "
"installable, works offline · English only"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Three providers normalised into one shape, so a Swiss bulletin and an "
"Austrian one are rendered by the same model rather than as two different "
"documents, down to each problem's aspects and elevation band. It is a "
"website rather than an app: nothing to install, and it keeps working without "
"signal once a page has loaded. It shows the provider's bulletin in full "
"rather than a summary, on the view that deciding what to leave out of an "
"avalanche forecast is not ours to do."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Around the bulletin: a slope-angle layer for Switzerland, derived from "
"swisstopo's swissALTI3D elevation model and drawn in the same band "
"classification as the Swiss maps, the national topographic basemaps for "
"Switzerland, France and Austria, mountain weather forecasts refreshed four "
"times a day, uploaded routes, and shared trips."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"<strong class=\"text-text-1\">Strongest at</strong> reading the same way "
"across four countries, on a phone, offline, with nothing to install — and at "
"planning a day with other people. <strong class=\"text-text-1\">Weakest at</"
"strong> alerting, which it does not do at all; observed weather-station "
"readings, where it has forecasts but no live station network; per-tour risk "
"scoring, which it deliberately does not do — a trip page states where your "
"line enters the ground each problem names, and stops short of rating the "
"day; languages other than English; and teaching material, where WhiteRisk is "
"far ahead. If you ski only in Switzerland, WhiteRisk is the better product "
"and it is not close."
msgstr ""

msgid "Tools that rate a specific tour"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Günter Schmudlach (CH) and the Petzl Foundation (FR) · free · checked 30 Aug "
"2026"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Two sibling products answering a narrower and more useful question than "
"\"what does the bulletin say\": given today's bulletin and the terrain a "
"particular tour crosses, how risky is that tour today. Skitourenguru covers "
"the Alps generally and is Swiss-rooted; Yéti is the newer France-only "
"sibling pairing the Météo-France bulletin with terrain analysis from an "
"elevation model. Both were previously gated and are now free, over a "
"database of about 13,000 tours."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"<strong class=\"text-text-1\">Strongest at</strong> the single most decision-"
"relevant output in this whole list, free, over a large route database — and "
"the methodology is credible enough that a major tour portal now licenses it "
"as a map layer. <strong class=\"text-text-1\">Weakest at</strong> coverage: "
"no one product spans the whole Alps, and funding rests on a foundation "
"rather than revenue, which is a different kind of risk from a subscription."
msgstr ""

msgid "Planning platforms"
msgstr ""

msgid "Whympr, born in Chamonix · iOS, Android and web · checked 30 Aug 2026"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"An all-in-one mountain trip planner covering hiking, ski touring, "
"mountaineering, climbing, trail running and snowshoeing, in which the "
"avalanche bulletin is one tab. The draw is everything around it: over "
"100,000 routes and points of interest, eleven national topographic maps, 3D "
"and LiDAR terrain, slope-gradient shading across Europe, weather, 23,000 "
"webcams, and offline area downloads with your chosen map and layers."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Its terrain layers carry an explicit warning that a rating can shift a full "
"grade with season or weather, which is the correct framing for any such "
"layer."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"<strong class=\"text-text-1\">Strongest at</strong> being the only app you "
"need to open before a tour, if planning matters more to you than forecast "
"depth, and at community content by a wide margin. <strong class=\"text-"
"text-1\">Weakest at</strong> the bulletin itself, which is a thin pass-"
"through of the official product — no normalisation across providers, no "
"history, and nothing that reads the forecast for you."
msgstr ""

msgid "Weather-first platforms"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Cloudnine Weather, Inc., United States · iOS, Android and web · checked 30 "
"Aug 2026"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Included because it is the most commercially proven shape in the category, "
"not because it competes in the Alps — it does not. The question it answers "
"is where it will snow, not whether a slope will hold. Its spine is a human-"
"written daily forecast narrative per region by named local meteorologists. "
"People subscribe to a writer, which is a different business from selling "
"access to a data feed."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Around that sit threshold alerts you set yourself, side-by-side comparison "
"across several mountains, ten days of past snowfall and snow line per "
"mountain, and an in-house forecast model the company claims is materially "
"more accurate in mountain terrain than what it replaced."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"<strong class=\"text-text-1\">Strongest at</strong> editorial voice and "
"alerting — proof that in this category people pay for a person's read, not "
"raw data. <strong class=\"text-text-1\">Weakest at</strong> avalanche work, "
"which is a bolt-on: no European coverage and no bulletin normalisation. Its "
"pricing is the most aggressive here."
msgstr ""

msgid "Also worth knowing about"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Products that come up often but have not been checked to the same depth as "
"the profiles above. Treat these as pointers, not assessments."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"<strong class=\"text-text-1\">Skida</strong> (Norway) — a ski-touring app "
"gathering official bulletins from several national providers in one place "
"without altering their content, drawing each avalanche problem as an aspect-"
"and-elevation diagram."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"<strong class=\"text-text-1\">avalanche.report</strong> — the official web "
"front end for Austria and northern Italy, and the region map those tourers "
"already know."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"<strong class=\"text-text-1\">Varsom</strong> (Norway) — state-run "
"observations and forecasts at national scale, with slope-angle and runout "
"map layers for Norway and Svalbard."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"<strong class=\"text-text-1\">onX Backcountry</strong> (US and Canada) — no "
"Alps coverage, but the most careful terrain layer of any of these: a terrain-"
"exposure scale plus a distinct rating for ground with no avalanche exposure "
"at all, which frames the layer as ruling terrain safe rather than rating "
"everything else dangerous."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"<strong class=\"text-text-1\">Bergfex</strong> (Austria) — the default tour-"
"and-weather portal across Austria and Germany, with avalanche information "
"attached."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"<strong class=\"text-text-1\">Outdooractive and alpenvereinaktiv</strong> — "
"tour portals with alpine-club backing, carrying institutional trust in "
"Austria and Germany."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"<strong class=\"text-text-1\">Camptocamp and Skitour.fr</strong> — the "
"French community route corpora other apps source from, and where many French "
"tourers actually plan."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"<strong class=\"text-text-1\">Avalanche Canada and avalanche.org</strong> — "
"the North American forecast agencies, both with their own apps."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"<strong class=\"text-text-1\">FATMAP</strong> — the 3D terrain mapping "
"product, acquired by Strava and retired in October 2024. Listed so nobody "
"spends an evening looking for it."
msgstr ""

msgid "How this was checked"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Each profiled product was checked against its own website and store listing "
"in August 2026, and re-checked twice since."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Several of these sites block automated fetching, so some claims are "
"corroborated across independent secondary sources rather than read from the "
"product itself. The two that matter most are OpenSnow's prices and "
"AvalancheClarity's coverage and cost. Check both yourself before paying for "
"anything."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A feature not described here may still exist. These notes record what was "
"found, and a product that is not said to do something may simply never have "
"been asked — which matters most in the planning and community section, where "
"features are often buried rather than advertised."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Coverage figures describe bulletins a product ingests or links to, not map "
"coverage, which is usually wider and is not the same promise."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This is a snapshot of a category that moves fast. In the last year alone "
"WhiteRisk added a second country's bulletin, SnowSafe added push alerts, and "
"Skitourenguru and Yéti became free. Anything here can be out of date by the "
"time you read it — tell us if it is."
msgstr ""

msgid "Disclosure"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This page is published by Snowdesk, which is one of the products listed on "
"it. We have tried to be useful rather than flattering — the guide above "
"sends you to WhiteRisk for Switzerland, to SnowSafe for Austria, and to "
"Skitourenguru or Yéti for a scored tour, and Snowdesk's own entry lists what "
"it does not do. Read it with the conflict in mind anyway."
msgstr ""

#, python-format
msgid ""
"Nothing here is a recommendation about whether to go into avalanche terrain. "
"The official bulletin from your national avalanche warning service is the "
"authority, and the decision is always yours to make with training, equipment "
"and partners. If you are new to reading one, start with <a "
"class=\"underline\" href=\"%(bulletin_guide_url)s\">how to read a bulletin</"
"a>."
msgstr ""

msgid "Help"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Plain-language guide to how Snowdesk works: the map, bulletins, favourites, "
"and more."
msgstr ""

msgid "Help & how it works"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A plain-language guide to what Snowdesk does and how to use each part of it."
msgstr ""

