msgid "Your account and this device"
msgstr ""

msgid "Accounts"
msgstr ""

msgid "Install & offline"
msgstr ""

msgid "Sync log"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Snowdesk accounts are passwordless by default: enter your email address and "
"follow the link that's sent to you &mdash; no password to remember. You can "
"optionally set a password, or register a passkey on your device, from the "
"account setup page."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"An account holds the things you save: the places and regions you pin, the "
"routes you upload, the trips you plan, and the map areas you download to "
"read offline. All of it is private to you, and everything except a field "
"observation stays that way. Snowdesk sends no bulletin alerts &mdash; the "
"only email it will send you is a sign-in link, a password reset, or a "
"confirmation that you changed your address."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To hear about a new bulletin, subscribe a feed reader to the RSS feed for "
"the country you follow: snowdesk.info/ch/feed.rss for Switzerland, and the "
"same address with at, it or fr for Austria, Italy and France. The feeds need "
"no account and list each bulletin as it is published."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"&ldquo;Highlight problem terrain&rdquo;, under Conditions in the layers "
"menu, paints every region&rsquo;s ground of 30 degrees and steeper by what "
"that region&rsquo;s own bulletin says about it that day: where a "
"problem&rsquo;s aspects and heights take in a slope, the slope is painted in "
"the colour of that problem&rsquo;s danger level. Where two problems overlap, "
"the higher level wins, and a day with a morning and an afternoon bulletin "
"shows the worse of the two. It paints from zoom 12, and where it paints, a "
"region&rsquo;s own danger colour steps aside. The switch of the same name "
"above a region&rsquo;s bulletin is the same setting: switching it on there "
"turns it on for the whole map, and it stays on as you move from region to "
"region."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"<strong>Grey hatching is not &ldquo;safe&rdquo;.</strong> Steep ground that "
"no problem describes is hatched in warm grey rather than left blank. A "
"bulletin&rsquo;s aspects and heights mark where a problem is most "
"pronounced, not a line beyond which it stops, so much of the steep ground on "
"any day falls outside them and can still slide."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Some problems cannot be placed slope by slope. One bounded by the treeline "
"has no height we can test, and one whose aspects come only from the "
"bulletin&rsquo;s text cannot be read as precisely as the structured ones. "
"Ground such a problem may describe is painted a flat grey, &ldquo;described, "
"can&rsquo;t be placed&rdquo;, rather than as a match or a miss."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The terrain shows from zoom level 12, roughly a valley across the screen; "
"further out the map shows each region&rsquo;s danger colour as usual, and "
"switching it on from a region&rsquo;s panel takes the map there. Ground the "
"terrain data has nothing for is hatched in slate, the other way from the "
"grey."
msgstr ""

msgid "What it cannot tell you"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"It does not show runout zones. An avalanche released on painted ground can "
"run far onto gentle ground below, which is never painted."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"It shows where the bulletin describes its problems, not a hazard map and "
"never a verdict on whether to ski a slope. Read the bulletin under the "
"switch; the layer is a way of finding the slopes it is talking about."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"It covers Switzerland only, because the terrain data is swisstopo&rsquo;s "
"swissALTI3D elevation model. A region elsewhere has no switch and keeps its "
"danger colour."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Every bulletin carries a danger rating on the five-level European scale "
"&mdash; Low, Moderate, Considerable, High and Very High. The number and its "
"colour are the headline: they say how likely avalanches are and how serious "
"they would be, for the region as a whole."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The panel below the header breaks the day into windows. Most days have a "
"single, unlabelled row: that rating holds all day and there is nothing more "
"to say. When a provider expects conditions to change &mdash; a storm "
"arriving, or the sun working on the snow &mdash; a second row appears with "
"its own rating and a \"Later\" tag, as above. Only a window that departs "
"from the baseline is tagged, so a tag on the panel always means the day is "
"going somewhere."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Some providers split a window by height instead, giving one rating above a "
"pivot elevation and another below it. Those rows carry a small mountain "
"glyph shaded to show which half of the mountain they describe."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For a full walkthrough of the scale and its subdivisions, see the dedicated "
"guide:"
msgstr ""

msgid "How to read a bulletin"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Every bulletin page has a \"Season\" button in its header, marked with the "
"season's years. It opens a calendar of the whole season for that region: one "
"dot per day from the season's start, each month a row &mdash; or, on a "
"phone, a column of short bars. The days are grouped in fives, so the 13th is "
"the third day after the gap marked 11."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Each day carries its danger rating in its colour, so a season's worth of "
"conditions reads at a glance &mdash; the settled spells, and the weeks that "
"ran high. A day that held two levels is split in half, the lower on the left "
"and the highest it reached on the right. Days still to come are drawn faint."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Tap any day that has a bulletin to jump straight to it; the day you are "
"reading is ringed. Days with no bulletin &mdash; before the season began, or "
"a gap in what the provider published &mdash; are drawn as an empty outline "
"and do nothing."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A download saves an area onto this device &mdash; the background map, and "
"the avalanche bulletins and weather inside it &mdash; so it still works "
"where there is no signal. The framed-square button on the right of the map "
"opens \"Your downloads\": everything you have stored, the space it takes, "
"and the way to add more."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"There are three ways to store an area. Select a region on the map and use "
"the download button beside its name in the header to take the whole region. "
"Use \"Download a custom area\" inside the sheet to frame any part of the map "
"yourself &mdash; the right choice for a tour that straddles a boundary. Or "
"tap the locate button and, once it has found you, tap it again to drop a "
"zone: a circle of map around where you are standing, sized by zooming in or "
"out. Each row in the sheet says which kind it is, and custom areas and drop "
"zones can be renamed."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A budget at the top of the sheet caps how much space downloads may take, and "
"you choose the figure. When a new download would take you past it, the "
"oldest areas are removed to make room and you are asked first. Lowering the "
"budget below what you already hold puts you over it, and the sheet says so "
"rather than deleting anything behind your back."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Starting a download needs an account. The controls stay where they are when "
"you are signed out and take you to the sign-in page instead; anything you "
"have already downloaded keeps working exactly as before, signed in or not, "
"because reading a stored map needs no connection and no account."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Your areas follow your account, but the downloaded maps do not. The sheet "
"lists what you hold in two groups, \"On this device\" and \"Not on this "
"device\". An area you saved on a laptop is listed on your phone under \"Not "
"on this device\", dimmed and with no size, because the space it takes is "
"counted only on the device that actually holds it &mdash; a listed area is "
"not the same thing as an available one. \"Download here\", in that row's "
"\"&hellip;\" menu, fetches the same area onto the phone."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The first time you open the map on a new device while signed in and online, "
"a notice tells you how many of your downloads aren't on it; \"Show "
"downloads\" opens the sheet. When two or more areas can be fetched here, "
"\"Download all\" heads the \"Not on this device\" group and downloads them "
"one after another with the basemap on screen. Nothing downloads until you "
"tap it, and it stops if you go offline or an area doesn't fit."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The downloaded maps live in this browser on this device. Clearing the site's "
"data or resetting local data removes them, and Remove, in a row's \"&hellip;"
"\" menu, removes that download from this device only. The area stays in your "
"list under \"Not on this device\", so you can download it again. To take it "
"off your account, use Forget on that row; a device that still has the area "
"downloaded will add it back."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"\"Display downloaded areas\", under Basemap in the layers menu &mdash; or "
"the \"Show on the map\" switch at the foot of the downloads panel, which is "
"the same setting &mdash; shades the areas you hold, so you can see the edges "
"of your own coverage before you set off. Left alone it switches itself on "
"whenever you are offline, which is when the answer matters; set it either "
"way and your choice stands."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"An area is downloaded for the basemap that was showing at the time, and each "
"row names it. Switching to a different basemap is switching to a map you "
"have not stored. A region holds one basemap at a time: its download button "
"then offers to replace the stored copy with this basemap's, and asks before "
"it does. A custom area or drop zone you save again under another basemap is "
"a second, separate download."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Downloading needs a connection; listing and removing what you already hold "
"works offline, which is when space usually runs short. A download can also "
"fail because the device is out of room or the area is larger than the budget "
"allows &mdash; remove other downloads or raise the budget, or, for a custom "
"area or drop zone, make it smaller and try again."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A download stores the avalanche bulletins and the weather for the ground "
"inside it as well as the map, and \"Sync now\" in a row's \"&hellip;\" menu "
"brings them up to date before you set off. To do all of it in one press, "
"open the connection symbol at the top of the page and choose \"Get ready for "
"offline\": it sends any changes still waiting on this device, then brings "
"every download up to date in turn, and each area says how it came out. Pages "
"outside your downloads are kept for offline use as you visit them &mdash; "
"see \"Install &amp; offline\" below."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Favourites let you pin specific spots &mdash; a trailhead, a car park, a "
"favourite line &mdash; directly on the map. Sign in, open the star button on "
"the right of the map, and use \"Add a favourite\": the map moves under a "
"fixed pin, so you place it with one hand."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"That same panel lists the pins you have dropped. Rename a pin in place, "
"remove one, and use the \"Show on the map\" switch at the foot to draw them "
"all &mdash; it is the same setting as the Favourites row in the layers menu, "
"so turning one moves the other."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To pin a resort, open it and press the star in its header. A pinned resort "
"turns blue on the map, and search lists it under \"Pinned resorts\" before "
"you type anything, beside the regions you have pinned."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Every favourite also has its own page you can bookmark or share &mdash; open "
"it from \"My favourites\" or from \"Your favourites here\" on that "
"favourite's region bulletin page."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Favourites are private: they are yours alone and are never shown to other "
"users. A pin dropped with no connection is saved on the device and sent when "
"you are back online."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Snowdesk can be installed as an app on your phone or computer. Look for an "
"\"Install\" or \"Add to Home Screen\" option in your browser's address bar "
"or share menu &mdash; once installed, it opens in its own window without any "
"browser chrome."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Pages you have already visited load instantly on repeat visits, and keep "
"working with no connection, because both the app and the data behind those "
"pages are kept on your device. Snowdesk always asks the network first and "
"only falls back to what it holds."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"That means a rating you see offline is the last one this device fetched, not "
"necessarily the one in force. The signal symbol at the top of every page "
"tells you which it is: struck through whenever the connection is failing, or "
"has failed often enough that Snowdesk has stopped trying and is serving "
"stored data only. Tap it for how long ago this device last reached the "
"server. Check that before you trust a rating, and reload once you have "
"signal."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can also stop Snowdesk using the network yourself. Tap the signal symbol "
"at the top of any page and turn on \"Offline mode\" &mdash; before you lose "
"coverage, or to spare a metered or roaming connection &mdash; and the app "
"runs from what it has already stored. It stays on until you turn it off "
"again: Snowdesk will not quietly reconnect. While it is on, that symbol is "
"struck through, much like aeroplane mode on a phone, and the same menu tells "
"you how old the stored data is and offers the way back."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The background map is the exception: it is stored only for the areas you "
"download yourself &mdash; see \"Offline downloads\" above."
msgstr ""

#, python-format
msgid ""
"To see exactly what this device has saved &mdash; and to clear it if "
"something is stuck &mdash; open <a href=\"%(url)s\" "
"class=\"underline\">Offline content</a>. You do not need an account, and it "
"is worth checking before a trip rather than after."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The stacked-squares button on the right of the map opens the layers menu "
"&mdash; one list controlling everything the map draws, grouped by what each "
"row is. A row you switch on stays on the next time you open Snowdesk on this "
"device."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Resorts draws a pin at every ski resort Snowdesk knows about. Tapping one "
"opens a short popup &mdash; the resort's name, the region it sits in, a link "
"to that region's bulletin, and a star to save it to your favourites."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Favourites draws your own saved places, and Routes draws every track you "
"have uploaded. Both need an account: tapping one while you are signed out "
"offers you the way in rather than switching on a layer with nothing in it."
msgstr ""

msgid "Conditions"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"One row per bulletin provider &mdash; SLF (CH), Météo-France (FR) and ALBINA "
"(AT, IT) &mdash; each covering the countries that provider publishes for. "
"Switching one off takes its bulletins off the map, which is the way to look "
"at one country's picture on its own. Switzerland is on to start with."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Under the three providers, \"Bulletin opacity\" sets how strongly the danger "
"colour paints: five steps from off to full, so you can read the terrain "
"underneath without losing the bulletin altogether."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Weather draws a condition symbol and the day's high temperature at every "
"place Snowdesk holds, with the height that reading is for beneath it. It "
"follows the timeline like everything else on the map, and it has rules of "
"its own worth knowing about &mdash; read \"Weather on the map\" below."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Field observations draws what other people have reported from the ground, "
"fading as each report ages. It is public, so it works whether or not you are "
"signed in."
msgstr ""

msgid "Boundaries"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Outlines of the European avalanche regions at three scales: Major (EAWS "
"Level 1), Minor (EAWS L2) and Micro (EAWS L4). Micro is the level bulletins "
"are issued at and is drawn by default; the two coarser outlines help you "
"place a small region inside the wider area it belongs to. They follow the "
"base map rather than the Bulletins rows: on a national base map you see that "
"country's regions, and on OpenFreeMap you see all of them."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The map underneath everything else, and the only section where you pick one "
"rather than switching several on. OpenFreeMap covers the whole area; the "
"national mapping-agency maps &mdash; Swisstopo (CH), IGN (FR) and basemap.at "
"(AT) &mdash; carry far more detail for their own country and are blank "
"outside it, so switching to one is a choice to look closely at one place."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Two things are drawn on top of whichever one you chose, which is why they "
"sit under this heading. The slope layer, which shades the ground by "
"steepness, lives inside \"Terrain filter\": choose &ldquo;By slope&rdquo; in "
"the panel its adjust button opens. It is the layer with the most real limits "
"on what it can tell you, so read \"Slope angle\" below before you rely on a "
"shading."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"\"Display downloaded areas\" shades the ground you already hold on this "
"device, so you can see the edges of your own coverage before you set off. "
"Left alone it switches itself on whenever you are offline, which is when the "
"answer matters."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"\"Terrain filter\" turns on a layer that highlights ground by aspect, slope "
"range and, if you like, a band of elevation; the adjust button beside it "
"opens the panel where you choose them. Read \"Terrain filter\" below for "
"what it does not tell you."
msgstr ""

msgid "The dot on each row"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Most rows carry a small dot saying whether that layer is held on this "
"device: green for available offline, grey for not stored yet, and a pulse "
"while it is being fetched. Green means the data is on the device right now, "
"checked rather than assumed, so you can see what will still work before you "
"lose signal. Two rows have no dot, because neither could answer honestly: "
"the terrain filter draws from tiles kept as you look at the map, so what is "
"held depends on where you have been, and your downloaded areas are on this "
"device by definition."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A row is sometimes switched off with a reason attached &mdash; a layer that "
"has never been loaded while you were online, for instance. The note on the "
"row says why."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Each heading opens and closes, and remembers which way you left it. The line "
"under a heading says what is on inside it without you having to open it, and "
"the count at the top of the menu says how many layers are on altogether."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The map colours every region by its avalanche danger level for the date "
"being shown, from pale green (Low) through to red (Very High). Tap a region "
"to select it: its name and that day's rating appear in the header above the "
"map, along with a link to the full bulletin."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The magnifier searches regions and resorts by name and jumps straight to "
"one. The crosshair centres the map on where you are, if you let your browser "
"share your position."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The buttons down the right-hand side are the map's controls: layers, "
"bulletin shading strength, offline downloads, your favourites, your field "
"observations and your routes. The chevron at the foot folds them away when "
"you want a clear map, and the \"?\" above it replays a short tour that names "
"every one of them."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The (i) button in the bottom-left corner opens the legend: the danger scale, "
"what each kind of pin means, the slope-angle bands when that layer is on, "
"and the attribution for the map and the avalanche data."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The number at the top of the bottom-left buttons is the map's zoom level. "
"Tap it for zoom-out and zoom-in buttons, if you would rather not pinch or "
"scroll, and a scale showing where you are between the widest view and the "
"closest. Some layers only appear once you are zoomed in far enough: the "
"terrain filter and the slope layer from level 12, and a route's slope "
"colours from level 14. While one is switched on, the scale marks the level "
"it starts at."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Your saved favourite pins can be drawn on the map alongside the resorts "
"layer &mdash; see \"Favourites\" below."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A community-reports overlay shows anonymised, publicly-shared field "
"observations from other Snowdesk users on the map."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The field-observations button lets you submit conditions you have seen in "
"person &mdash; see \"Field observations\" below."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Field observations are quick, one-tap reports of what you have actually seen "
"out on the snow. You pick one signal &mdash; whumpfing, shooting cracks, "
"fractures, wind striations or pinwheels &mdash; and that, with where and "
"when you saw it, is the whole report."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Sign in with a verified email address, then open the triangle button on the "
"right of the map. The panel lists your own reports and offers \"Report an "
"observation\". The \"Show on the map\" switch at its foot draws the "
"community's reports; it is the same setting as the \"Field observations\" "
"row in the layers menu."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The report takes your position from your device where it can, and you can "
"nudge the pin before sending. If there is no GPS fix, move the map to set "
"the spot yourself."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Unlike your pins and routes, reports are shared. They appear to other users "
"on the community-reports overlay and on the Observations page &mdash; "
"without your name, but with the place and time you recorded. Only report "
"what you are content to make public."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A report made with no connection is held on your device and sent as soon as "
"you have one, which is the point &mdash; the ground you are reporting on "
"rarely has signal."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Snowdesk brings together official avalanche bulletins from three providers "
"&mdash; SLF (Switzerland), ALBINA (Austria/EUREGIO), and Météo-France "
"&mdash; onto a single interactive map, with a consistent layout for every "
"region and every day."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You don't need an account to browse: pick a region on the map, read today's "
"danger rating and avalanche problems, or scrub back through the season to "
"see how conditions have evolved. Signing in adds the things you save: pinned "
"places and regions, uploaded routes, and map areas downloaded to read "
"offline."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The danger rating says how dangerous the day is. The avalanche problems say "
"why, and where. Each one is a card like the example above, and a bulletin "
"usually carries one to three of them."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The icon and title name the problem &mdash; fresh wind slab, a persistent "
"weak layer buried in the snowpack, wet snow, gliding snow, new snow or "
"cornices. Each behaves differently and asks for a different response, which "
"is why the bulletin names them rather than giving you a number alone."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Below the title, the compass rose and the elevation glyph say where the "
"problem lives: which slope aspects it has formed on, and above or below "
"which height. A problem on north-through-south-east aspects above 2200 "
"metres is not a problem on a sunny south-west slope at the valley floor, and "
"the card is telling you where to expect it."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The comment underneath is the forecaster's own words, shown as they wrote "
"them. It is the part that says what will release the problem and how it will "
"behave, and it is worth reading in full &mdash; Snowdesk never summarises it "
"away."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For what each problem type means in the snowpack, and how to read the cards "
"in detail, see:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Routes are your own tracks, drawn on the map beside the avalanche picture. "
"Sign in, open the routes button on the right of the map, and upload a .gpx "
"file containing a track or a route &mdash; the kind of file a GPS watch, a "
"planning tool or a touring app exports."
msgstr ""

#, python-format
msgid ""
"The file you exported is not always the file you want on the map. <a "
"href=\"%(editor_url)s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"text-"
"status-info-text underline\" >gpx.studio ↗</a> opens a .gpx in the browser "
"and lets you crop its ends, slice it into pieces, join two together or "
"redraw a stretch, then export the result &mdash; enough to take the drive "
"home off a recorded track, or to cut a multi-day tour into a day each. It is "
"free and needs no account."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The panel lists everything you have uploaded, each row with its distance "
"and, where the file recorded them, its ascent, its descent and how long the "
"recording ran. The button at the end of a row opens the things you can do "
"with it &mdash; plan a trip, share it, rename it or remove it. The \"Show on "
"the map\" switch at the foot of the panel, or the Routes row in the layers "
"menu &mdash; one setting, two places &mdash; draws them all as lines."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Tapping a line on the map opens the route's own popup: its name, the same "
"figures, and an elevation profile of the whole track. The profile is drawn "
"from the heights in your file, so a track recorded without them shows none "
"rather than a flat line. The time shown is elapsed &mdash; start to finish, "
"including every stop &mdash; not moving time, and a file with no timestamps "
"in it shows none."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Routes are private. They are visible only to you, they are never shown to "
"other users, and they play no part in the community-reports overlay."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Nothing is published, but you can hand one route to someone else. The Share "
"control on a route&apos;s row gives you a link to send them; whoever opens "
"it sees that route drawn on their map with a Save button. They can open the "
"link before they have signed in, and it will still be waiting once they have."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Saving takes a copy. Their copy is theirs to rename or remove and yours is "
"untouched, so the two never affect each other afterwards. The link keeps "
"working for anyone you send it to for a few weeks, and it stops working "
"straight away if you remove the route it points at."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Uploading needs a connection, as do renaming and removing &mdash; those "
"actions are not queued for later the way a field observation is. There is a "
"limit on the size of a single file and on how many routes one account can "
"keep; the panel says so when you reach either. A very long track is thinned "
"before it is stored, which keeps it quick to draw and leaves its shape, "
"distance and profile intact."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Once your routes have loaded on a device, the lines stay on the map with no "
"connection. The list inside the panel needs one, and says so instead of "
"pretending you have no routes."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The slope layer shades the ground by steepness, so you can see how steep a "
"line is without leaving the map. The bands follow the classification the SLF "
"recommends, the same one you will have seen on Swiss maps: 30&ndash;35&deg;, "
"35&ndash;40&deg;, 40&ndash;45&deg;, 45&ndash;50&deg; and over 50&deg;. "
"Ground under 30&deg; is left unshaded."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Turn it on from the layers menu: tick &ldquo;Terrain filter&rdquo;, open its "
"settings with the adjust button beside it and choose &ldquo;By slope&rdquo;. "
"The rest of that panel still applies. The slope range says which bands are "
"painted, from 30&deg; up unless you change it, and the aspects and the "
"elevation band narrow it further &mdash; so you can colour only the north-"
"facing slopes above 2,400 metres, say, by how steep they are."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The first three bands matter most. The large majority of slab avalanches "
"release on slopes between 30 and 45 degrees, which is why that range is the "
"one to find when a bulletin talks about steep terrain in a particular aspect "
"and elevation."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A saved route&rsquo;s steepness is read on the map: zoom in close and the "
"route line itself is coloured in the same classes, one colour for each "
"stretch of the same class; on a trip&rsquo;s page the line is coloured the "
"same way. Either way the colour is the steepness of the GROUND, never of the "
"track: a skin track zigzagging up a steep face climbs gently along its own "
"length, and colouring it by its own gradient would make a serious slope look "
"easy. Where the line is grey and dashed, the terrain data does not cover it "
"&mdash; that is the same coverage limit described below, and it never means "
"the ground is gentle."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The same rule runs the other way, and it surprises people: a flat traverse "
"can be drawn steep. A path cut across a face &mdash; a piste traverse, a "
"summer track, a bench &mdash; is level underfoot while the ground it crosses "
"is not, and the elevation model describes the hillside rather than the few "
"metres you walk on. So a level stretch of a tour can carry 40 or 50 degree "
"colours. That is the slope above and below you, and on a traverse it is the "
"number that matters."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Tap the route, or the top line of its profile, to read one point. A point is "
"a 25 metre segment of the track: zoomed in close, the map marks that segment "
"itself, in its colour, with square ends where it starts and stops, and "
"further out it shows a dot. The profile marks it with a dot and gives its "
"height and its distance from the start; tap the wheel to clear it. The route "
"panel&rsquo;s header, just above the profile, gives two things: how steep "
"the track is there and how it crosses the slope &mdash; down or up the fall "
"line, or across it, and which side the slope falls &mdash; and how steep the "
"ground is, in the words the avalanche services use &mdash; moderate under 30 "
"degrees, steep from 30, very steep from 35 and extremely steep from 40. "
"Ground under 5 degrees reads as flat. The two can differ a great deal: a "
"track crossing an extremely steep face can itself be almost level. A track "
"can change direction within a single point, as it does at a kick turn on a "
"zigzag: the header then names the direction it starts and ends in, joined by "
"an arrow, and how steep the track is can change from one point to the next."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The colours are worked out by Snowdesk from swissALTI3D, the elevation model "
"of Switzerland published by swisstopo, the Swiss Federal Office of "
"Topography. Its heights are resampled to a 5 metre grid, and the steepness "
"of each point is measured across the ground around it. The route line&rsquo;"
"s colours and the figures in the route panel are worked out from the same "
"heights in the same way, so the layer and a route drawn over it agree about "
"the same hillside."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Each point&rsquo;s steepness is measured across about 30 metres of hillside, "
"so the shading describes the average slope of the ground around a point "
"rather than the ground itself. A short steep roll inside an otherwise gentle "
"face will not appear at any zoom, and neither will a small bench in the "
"middle of a steep one. Zoom in far enough and you will see individual "
"squares of about 7 metres &mdash; that is the resolution of the data, not a "
"fault in the map."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"It fades out when you zoom out. The layer is drawn from zoom level 12, "
"roughly a valley across the screen; further out nothing is drawn, and the "
"sheet asks you to zoom in. An empty map at a wide view says nothing about "
"how steep the ground is."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"It stops at the Swiss border. swissALTI3D covers Switzerland, so across the "
"border in France, Italy, Austria or Germany the layer has nothing to show. "
"Near the border, ground the data does not reach is hatched in grey; past the "
"edge of the data altogether the sheet says there is no terrain data here. "
"Either way, unshaded ground there has not been surveyed and is not ground "
"under 30 degrees."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Treat the layer as one input among several, never as a verdict. It says "
"nothing about the snowpack, about what is above you, or about the day's "
"danger rating &mdash; and a slope being shaded, or not being shaded, is not "
"permission to ski it."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The elevation data is swisstopo&rsquo;s swissALTI3D, resampled and derived "
"by Snowdesk. Full attribution is in the colophon."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Tapping the signal symbol at the top of the page names one clock: when this "
"device last reached the server. It also says which state you are in &mdash; "
"requests are still being attempted and will update the page as soon as one "
"lands, or Snowdesk has stopped calling the network and is serving stored "
"data until you tap \"Try reconnecting\"."
msgstr ""

#, python-format
msgid ""
"The <a href=\"%(url)s\" class=\"underline\">Offline content</a> page has a "
"\"Sync log\" panel listing your device's most recent real syncs with the "
"server (cached, offline-served pages don't count). It keeps the newest 100 "
"entries."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"\"Reset local data\" on the same page clears the sync log along with "
"everything else cached on this device."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The terrain filter highlights ground by the three things a bulletin uses to "
"say where the danger is: the aspect a slope faces, how steep it is, and how "
"high it is. Choose any of the eight aspects, a slope range from 30 degrees "
"upward, and if you like a band of elevation, and the map tints the ground "
"that matches in blue. Ground that does not match is left as it is. The "
"layers menu&rsquo;s &ldquo;Terrain filter&rdquo; row is a checkbox that "
"turns it on and off; the adjust button beside it opens the settings as a "
"panel, and turns the filter on if it is off. While it is on, that row and "
"the map&rsquo;s legend say what it is set to."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The panel has two ways to colour what matches. &ldquo;One tint&rdquo; tints "
"it blue; &ldquo;By slope&rdquo; colours it by how steep it is, in the same "
"bands as the Swiss slope maps &mdash; that is the map&rsquo;s slope layer, "
"described under &ldquo;Slope angle&rdquo;. The aspects, slope range and "
"elevation band apply to both."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The filter shows from zoom level 12, roughly a valley across the screen; "
"zoom out further and its row in the layers menu asks you to zoom in."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"<strong>Hatched means no data.</strong> Where the terrain data has nothing, "
"the ground is hatched in grey, so an untinted slope always means &ldquo;does "
"not match&rdquo; and never &ldquo;not surveyed&rdquo;. Level ground, such as "
"a lake, faces no direction and never matches."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"It describes the terrain and nothing else. It says nothing about the "
"snowpack, the weather or the day&rsquo;s danger rating, and a tinted slope "
"is not one that will avalanche, any more than an untinted one is safe."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"It does not show runout zones. An avalanche released on a matching slope can "
"run far onto ground that does not match &mdash; a gentle valley floor below "
"a steep north face is untinted and still in the path."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Treat it as a way of finding the slopes a bulletin is talking about, never "
"as a verdict on whether to ski them."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"It covers Switzerland only. The terrain data is swisstopo&rsquo;s "
"swissALTI3D elevation model, and outside Switzerland the filter has nothing "
"to show."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The scrubber along the bottom of the map moves it through the season, a day "
"at a time. Drag the thumb, and every region's colour changes to its danger "
"rating for the date you land on."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The buttons either side of the track work like a media player: skip to the "
"start or the end of the season, or play the whole thing as a timelapse, "
"forwards or backwards. A second press stops it."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The date sits in a pill above the track, and everything else on the map "
"follows it &mdash; the danger colours, the region readout in the header, and "
"the weather symbols when that layer is on."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The weather layer is the one thing that does not follow the whole season. It "
"is a forecast, so it covers today and the week ahead: scrub back past today "
"and the symbols go, while the danger colours stay."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Resort and saved-pin pages carry the day's weather: a condition icon and "
"label, the high and low temperature, any new snow, the freezing level, wind, "
"and when the sun rises and sets. It comes from Open-Meteo and describes one "
"place. A bulletin covers a whole region rather than a place, so it carries "
"no Open-Meteo reading &mdash; its weather is the forecaster's own "
"description, further down the bulletin page under \"Snowpack & Weather\"."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A resort page shows one block per place we hold for it &mdash; typically the "
"village, a mid-mountain station and the top &mdash; each labelled with its "
"height. That is the point of showing more than one: rain in the village and "
"snow on the summit are the same forecast read at two altitudes, and the "
"difference is usually what decides the day."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Each of those places links to its own forecast page, and that is where the "
"week lives: one column per day with its icon, temperatures, wind and "
"freezing level. The first few days also carry an hour-by-hour breakdown and "
"the later ones do not, because the forecast is not published at that "
"resolution that far out."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Past dates show no weather at all. We record what the forecast said on each "
"day going forward, so there is nothing on file for days before we started "
"keeping it. A bulletin page for last winter shows the bulletin and no "
"weather panel, rather than guessing."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The icon takes its day or night form from the time you are reading at, "
"matched against sunrise and sunset where the place actually is &mdash; so a "
"page read at midday looks like daytime whichever date you have navigated to."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The Weather row under \"Conditions\" in the layers menu puts a condition "
"symbol on the map at every place Snowdesk holds &mdash; resort villages, mid-"
"stations and summits, plus a point at the centre of each avalanche region. "
"Under each symbol is the day's high temperature and the height that reading "
"is for."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The height is there because the temperature means little without it. Two "
"degrees at 1500 m and two degrees at 3000 m describe different weeks, and on "
"a map that shows both at once the number alone would be misleading."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Zoomed out, places close together collapse to one symbol, and it is always "
"the lowest of them. A valley reading is the one you can place, and its "
"freezing level is what tells you whether it is raining where you would park. "
"Zoom in and the higher stations separate out again."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The overlay is a forecast, so it covers today and the week ahead and nothing "
"before that. Scrubbing the timeline back past today leaves the map with no "
"weather symbols; scrubbing forward changes them without fetching anything, "
"because the whole week arrives in one go."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Only curated places appear here. Your own saved pins are never added to this "
"layer, even for you &mdash; their weather lives on the pin's own card, where "
"nobody else can reach it."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Every trip has a meeting point: the spot the organiser dropped on the map "
"when they planned it. Where we can, it is shown as a three word address "
"&mdash; three words after a triple slash, like ///filled.count.soap. The "
"words are not a name anyone chose. They identify one 3-metre square of "
"ground anywhere in the world, so they say exactly what a pair of coordinates "
"says, in something you can read down a phone."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To find the square, type the three words into what3words or into a map app "
"that understands them. The organiser's page and the link they send you both "
"show the same address, so everyone on a trip is looking at the same words."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When we have no address for a meeting point, the page shows its latitude and "
"longitude instead &mdash; the same numbers it has always shown, ready to "
"paste into a map app. The meeting point is never blank."
msgstr ""

